@@ -331,6 +331,45 @@ These operations are command-only because calling them from lifecycle handlers c
 
 Session replacement invalidates the old context. Capture only plain data before switching, then use the fresh context supplied to `withSession` for session-bound work.
 
+### Cancellable idle actions
+
+An AgentSession-backed context, including an extension shortcut, optionally exposes `ctx.idleActions` with `version: 1`. Request one finite interaction from an event or shortcut; do not await its result from an awaited lifecycle handler. The callback starts only after relevant handlers, native controls, input operations and pending input have finished, including idle cache-warming decisions and their awaited provider headers/payload hooks. Shortcuts are not themselves an awaited lifecycle barrier and may await the result.
+
+Blocking leased UI currently requires a qualified local TUI (`ctx.mode === "tui"` with a real UI). RPC has no dialog-close acknowledgement: leased `select`, `confirm`, `input`, `editor` and `custom` calls fail before any UI request is sent. Such a callback fails with `callback_error` unless it handles the unsupported call. Metadata-only actions remain available in other modes. Ordinary RPC dialogs and their `hasUI` behavior are unchanged; `hasUI` alone does not qualify a leased dialog.
+
+```typescript
+const actions = ctx.idleActions;
+if (!actions || actions.version !== 1 || ctx.mode !== "tui" || !ctx.hasUI) return; // No legacy-idle fallback.
+const handle = actions.request({
+  id: attemptId, // A fresh UUID, retained for duplicate calls to this one attempt.
+  binding: materialHash, // Lowercase SHA-256 of source/request/material identity.
+  isCurrent: () => sourceStillMatches(), // Synchronous, read-only, literal boolean.
+  run: async (lease) => {
+    const selected = await lease.context.ui.select("Review complete?", ["Continue", "Later"], {
+      signal: lease.signal,
+    });
+    if (selected !== "Continue" || !lease.isCurrent()) return;
+    // Save any application approval through its original validated control here.
+    await lease.dispatch(
+      { customType: "extension-next-step", content: "Continue within the approved scope", display: false },
+      () => sourceStillMatches() && originalExecutionChecksPass(),
+    );
+  },
+});
+```
+
+The application supplies `attemptId`, `materialHash`, and its source/execution checks. A lease is not an approval, authentication proof, or execution budget. Saving an approval and dispatching a model turn remain separate operations. After saving, a cancelled or unknown dispatch must retain that fact, not collect approval again or automatically retry it.
+
+`state()` returns a lifecycle `phase` (`busy`, `settling`, `idle`), `pendingInputs`, and optional `activeActionId`. The pending count includes outstanding input operations and pending sources: the real core queue is one source, not the UI's steering/follow-up mirrors. It also includes deferred settled actions (even after removal from the deferred array), next-turn/context-only messages and pending bash records. Neither the old `isIdle()` nor a zero old `hasPendingMessages()` proves action eligibility.
+
+Only one pending/active attempt is admitted per session. Duplicate id/binding returns the original handle; a changed binding conflicts, and another id while occupied is rejected as busy. Up to 4096 attempt records are retained without eviction, after which new requests fail closed with capacity. Reload does not reset this bound. Attempts are not persisted or replayed on restart.
+
+The callback must use `lease.context`, pass `lease.signal` to its interaction, and close custom UI in an abort handler before returning. Its UI has an explicit owner token; ordinary event, EventBus and shortcut contexts never inherit ownership. Competing input or ordinary UI cancels an unstarted action and joins its cleanup before proceeding. A callback that fails to close its UI keeps the slot blocked; Pi does not force-kill arbitrary extension JavaScript. A cached context or UI method cannot acquire new rights after reload, replacement, or lease invalidation.
+
+`dispatch()` snapshots the message and is one-shot. Its read-only validator may be asynchronous, but cancellation releases the dispatch immediately and a late `true` cannot revive it. Only literal `true` passes. Ordinary `pi.sendMessage()`, `pi.sendUserMessage()`, input or native control calls from the action callback/validator fail fast instead of waiting on themselves. Ordinary observers, including AgentSession subscribers and runner error listeners, execute outside this host action marker while retaining their own extension async-local state. Their original synchronous return/exception semantics are unchanged.
+
+`handle.result` distinguishes completed, cancelled, rejected, failed, unknown and started outcomes; `handle.cancel()` cancels only unstarted delivery. Completed only means the callback ended. Started contains `actionId`, `runId` and `sessionId` and acknowledges the synchronous **logical start** where the session becomes busy, before preparation or a provider request. AgentSession subscribers additionally receive `idle_action_started` with those same identities. This is distinct from RPC's prompt acknowledgement and the later kernel `agent_start`; it does not mean provider/task success. Once acknowledged, later failures cannot make the attempt replayable. If preparation fails before kernel start, `agent_settled.idleActionFailure` identifies the acknowledged delivery so the application can hold it for inspection.
+
